      "heading": "Introduction",
      "section_type": "intro",
      "position": 3,
      "text": "Systematic reviews are a cornerstone of evidence-based medicine, providing structured syntheses that inform clinical practice and policy decisions [1,2]. Despite their importance, these reviews remain labour-intensive, particularly at the initial title-and- abstract screening stage, where researchers must manually exclude large numbers of irrelevant records. Studies estimate that screening alone can account for up to 60% of total review time, and over 75% of retrieved papers are eventually excluded [3,4]. The resulting workload contributes to reviewer fatigue, delays, and both intra- and inter- reviewer variation in decision-making [5]. Automation and machine learning tools have been introduced to alleviate this burden. Active-learning platforms such as Abstrackr and RobotAnalyst have shown moderate success in prioritising relevant studies [6,7]. However, traditional text-mining algorithms rely on surface-level keyword matching or supervised training data, which limits their generalisability across topics [8]. �����������������������������������������������, particularly the GPT series (e.g., GPT-��, have introduced new opportunities for natural-language reasoning, contextual understanding, and semantic classification [9-11]. Early evaluations suggest that LLMs can accurately summarise abstracts, identify study designs, and even emulate human screening decisions in systematic reviews [12-14]. Yet concerns persist regarding reproducibility, data transparency, and the potential for silent reasoning errors and hallucinations when models operate without human oversight [15, 16]. To address these challenges, we developed a hybrid Delphi-inspired expert–LLM workflow that combines expert consensus with automated evidence screening. This approach was implemented within the Thyroid Risk Stratification Tool (ThyRST) study, which investigates malignancy risk among thyroid nodules smaller than 30 mm. The proposed method integrates ChatGPT-based thematic classification with a Delphi-inspired consensus process among clinicians and data scientists to validate exclusion rules before large-scale application. This process followed the principles of structured expert consultation used in Delphi studies but was simplified into a single, consensus-based discussion. By quantifying concordance between LLM and human reviewers, we evaluate the feasibility of using generative AI to accelerate systematic- review workflows while maintaining transparency, traceability, and clinical relevance. 2.",
