      "heading": "Methods",
      "section_type": "methods",
      "position": 4,
      "text": "This study was conducted within the Thyroid Risk Stratification Tool (ThyRST) project, a multicentre collaboration between the University of Birmingham and University Hospitals Birmingham NHS Foundation Trust. The review investigated the risk of malignancy in thyroid nodules smaller than 30 mm, following the PICO framework of adult������������������������������������������������������������������������������ malignancy as the outcome. To have maximum sensitivity, a comprehensive searches were performed across PubMed, Embase, Cochrane Library, Scopus, Web of Science, and CINAHL, retrieving 14,858 records for screening. O. Pournik et al. / A Hybrid Delphi-Inspired Expert–LLM Workflow 671 All titles and abstracts were processed using the GPT-5 model via ChatGPT to provide an initial thematic classification into broad research categories, including clinical imaging, cytology, molecular or animal studies, and population or technical investigations. This exploration revealed that many records were irrelevant because they reported non-human or laboratory-only work. Recurring textual patterns from these irrelevant studies (for example, “rat model,” “cell line,” “radioiodine uptake” ������� extracted as potential exclusion clues. These clues were reviewed through a Delphi- inspired expert consensus discussion involving clinicians and data scientists, who assessed each clue for relevance, specificity, and risk of false exclusion to achieve consensus. The final agreed exclusion criteria covered non-human, laboratory-only, non- diagnostic, non-English, and paediatric-only studies. The confirmed exclusion rules were incorporated into structured ChatGPT prompts instructing the model to label each abstract as INCLUDE, EXCLUDE, or MAYBE, with a short justification. Outputs were exported as comma-separated files and merged with citation metadata for review. All data processing used pseudonymised text in accordance with the project’s approved data-governance framework. To assess accuracy, 100 randomly selected records were independently screened by two human reviewers blinded to the ChatGPT decisions. Concordance and reliability ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������� False-negative cases, defined as studies wrongly excluded by ChatGPT but included by human reviewers, were identified. Screening time per 100 abstracts was recorded to quantify workload reduction achieved through the hybrid Delphi-inspired expert–LLM workflow. 3.",
