      "text": "Application of the LLM–assisted workflow to the 14,858 retrieved records produced a complete automated classification within approximately two hours of model alignment and runtime. The initial ChatGPT-generated thematic mapping successfully distinguished clinical imaging and cytology studies from molecular, animal, and population-based publications, allowing the exclusion rules derived from the expert review process to be applied efficiently at scale. When validated against human reviewers, ChatGPT achieved 96% concordance ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� model; this study had been labelled “maybe” by a human reviewer and was ultimately judged borderline relevant. Conversely, two studies initially labelled as “include” by ChatGPT were later excluded during full-text screening, demonstrating that automated exclusion did not lead to the loss of relevant evidence. The hybrid workflow required a total of 1.7 hours for aligning and executing, excluding 93% (13890� of 14,858 records. The model’s outputs were consistent across different topic categories, and no systematic bias toward any research design or journal source was observed. Overall, these findings confirm that a carefully configured ChatGPT workflow, supported by expert-validated exclusion rules, can accurately and transparently remove irrelevant studies in large-scale systematic reviews while preserving human oversight and methodological traceability. O. Pournik et al. / A Hybrid Delphi-Inspired Expert–LLM Workflow 672 4.",
