      "text": "This study demonstrates that LLMs can substantially improve the efficiency of systematic review screening without compromising accuracy or transparency. Using GPT-5 via ChatGPT, supported by expert-validated exclusion rules, the workflow ���������������������������������������������������������������������������� screening time remarkably. These findings suggest the potential of generative AI to augment, rather than replace, human reviewers in evidence synthesis. Compared with conventional supervised machine-learning approaches, such as ������������������������������������������������������������������–0.8 [6,7], the present workflow required no pre-labelled training data or algorithmic tuning. Its success depended instead on structured prompting and expert input that ensured alignment with the review’s clinical context. The minimal number of false exclusions observed illustrates that automated first-pass triage can safely eliminate obviously irrelevant studies, allowing human reviewers to focus on borderline and included records. A Delphi-inspired structured, single-round expert consensus improved interpretability and trustworthiness by validation of exclusion criteria, ensuring clinical relevance, and minimising false exclusions. This Delphi-inspired design demonstrates a pragmatic model for combining expert insight and generative AI reasoning, providing a replicable approach for future evidence synthesis, ensuring the LLM’s reasoning reflected clinical logic, avoided bias toward lab-based or animal studies, and adhered to the PICO. Moreover, the transparency of the process, where prompts and exclusion rules are fully documented and reproduced, addresses concerns raised in literature regarding reproducibility and auditability of LLM outputs [9,14,15]. In summary, hybrid LLM-expert workflow was used, combining automated thematic clustering and abstract labelling with human-defined exclusion rules, expert resolution of borderline cases, and independent validation. To align with systematic review standards, all validation records were independently assessed by two human reviewers, with disagreements resolved through discussion. Despite these strengths, limitations remain. The approach was tested in a single clinical domain and with one model version, and further research is needed to evaluate cross-topic generalisability and performance stability across LLM updates. While the efficiency gains were clear, expert verification remains essential to ensure that no relevant evidence is lost. Integration of this approach within existing systematic-review management systems (e.g., Rayyan, ��������������������������������������nd support standardised validation at scale. 5.",
