      "position": 7,
      "text": "This study presents a practical and transparent workflow for combining expert consensus with LLM reasoning to improve efficiency in systematic reviews. Applied within this case study, the method achieved near-perfect agreement with human reviewers while reducing screening time. These results confirm that ChatGPT, when guided by structured prompting and expert-defined exclusion rules, can safely automate the first-pass triage of irrelevant studies without compromising accuracy or reproducibility. Unlike traditional machine-learning classifiers that rely on labelled training data, this approach is immediately deployable across new topics and domains, offering a scalable and auditable framework for integrating generative AI into evidence synthesis. The findings align with emerging literature highlighting both the promise and the necessary O. Pournik et al. / A Hybrid Delphi-Inspired Expert–LLM Workflow 673 safeguards for responsible AI adoption in health research [9, 15]. Despite low risk of false exclusion and observed workload reduction within a single clinical domain, further evaluation across diverse datasets and LLM platforms will be needed to confirm generalizability and will expand validation across multiple clinical domains and LLM platforms and explore direct integration with systematic-review management tools to further streamline transparent, human-in-the-loop screening pipelines.",
